  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max arrives at Victoria's apartment and awkwardly asks to move in with her. He tries to sound independent, but eventually admits his true feelings. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Привет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу переехать к тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе негде жить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, у меня миллион вариантов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя зовёт меня назад.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва тоже приглашает жить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я готов жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что ты хочешь сказать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не хочу быть без тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень приятно слышать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Правда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, я тебе верю.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi, Max.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'hi' straight to 'Max'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Привет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi.' In Russian, 'hi' leads into 'hi' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу переехать к тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to move to your place.' Here 'I' sets up the idea, while 'for you' comes at the end for emphasis. Then Vika replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе негде жить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have nowhere to live?' Here 'for you' sets up the idea, while 'to live' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, у меня миллион вариантов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I have a million options.' You'll hear 'me' all the time in the show, here connected with 'options'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя зовёт меня назад.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya is calling me back.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Kostya' paired with the verb for 'calls'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва тоже приглашает жить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Leva is also inviting me to live.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Lyova' paired with the adverb for 'too'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я готов жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But I am ready to live with you.' In Russian, 'I' leads into 'you' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что ты хочешь сказать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you want to say?' Vika starts with 'what' and pairs it with 'you' to make the point clear. Max answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to live with you.' A compact expression built around 'I' and 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не хочу быть без тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not want to be without you.' Listen for the word meaning 'I' right alongside 'want' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень приятно слышать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is very pleasant to hear.' In Russian, 'this' leads into 'to hear' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Правда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Really?' Notice the word order: 'honestly' comes first, and 'honestly' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, я тебе верю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I believe you.' Here 'I' sets up the idea, while 'believe' comes at the end for emphasis. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
